r sizes; the list is read when it opens and after each save, never every frame. A tap opens that save as the scene, which still saves to its own file, so the next Save makes it the current one and keeps the one it replaces: going back loses nothing. With changes the file does not hold, the first tap turns the row red ("Discard changes and open?") and a second tap within three seconds opens it, as New scene asks. The format is little-endian binary with a version number: `scene_save` in `src/app/files.odin`, then per object `levels_write` in `src/sculpt/levels_file.odin`. Since version 22 a scene starts, right after its version, with a **preview block**, as a `.blend` keeps its thumbnail, so a list of scenes can show each by reading only the start of its file, never the whole scene (also through Android's `content://` files): its length, when it was written, how many objects and vertices it holds, and a picture of the viewport as it was last drawn (without the brush ring or gizmo), at most 320 pixels on its long side, as a PNG (`src/app/scene_preview.odin`). The picture is taken while the app rests (a second after the last press, at most every two seconds, only when the scene or the view changed), copied back from the GPU without waiting for it and shrunk by averaging every pixel it covers, about 4.6 ms in a release build on the VM at 1600 x 900; a save brings it up to date first, and an autosave writes the last one taken. A length that runs past the file, or past 4 MB, is a damaged file; a picture that does not decode only shows none. The exit stats add `previews` (pictures taken), `preview_size`, `preview_kb` and `preview_ms`.
 
